(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-prognosis-outcomes-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Prognosis and Outcomes Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How can the natural history of a disease be changed to improve prognosis?","choices":["Through clinical intervention","Through longer observation alone","Through more precise measurement","Through larger study cohorts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the further aim is to detect disease at an earlier point so that treatment is as effective as it can be. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Of 240 people who have a disease, 18 die of it. What is the case-fatality ratio?","choices":["92.5 percent","13.3 percent","0.75 percent","7.5 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 18 deaths divided by 240 people with the disease is 0.075. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In the Kaplan-Meier method, what starts a new interval?","choices":["The end of each calendar year","The exact time each death occurs","The enrollment of each new patient","Every scheduled follow-up visit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and this is what generates the stepwise curve, in place of pre-determined intervals such as death at 1 year. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why are several prognostic factors used together?","choices":["Each factor alone predicts outcome perfectly","Together they remove the need to follow patients","Together they cancel every source of bias","Together they predict outcome better than one finding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and clinical prediction rules exploit this by combining a set of patient characteristics into one estimate. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the median survival time?","choices":["The average of everyone's survival time","The time until the last patient dies","The time half the population survives","The survival expected without the disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it can be read from the curve at the point where survival crosses 50 percent. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which finding worsens prognosis after an acute myocardial infarction?","choices":["Younger age","Congestive heart failure","Normal blood pressure","Never having smoked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, one of the poor prognostic factors, alongside ventricular arrhythmia, anterior infarction, hypotension and increasing age. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"The hazard ratio from a Cox analysis is interpreted like which measure?","choices":["Relative risk (risk ratio)","Sensitivity","Prevalence","Positive predictive value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a hazard ratio above 1 means more risk of the outcome than the comparison group, and below 1 means less. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the aim of detecting a disease at an earlier point?","choices":["To lengthen the preclinical phase","To maximize the effectiveness of treatment","To avoid ever needing follow-up","To raise the reported survival rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though earlier detection helps only if treatment given sooner actually changes the outcome, which is not automatic. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the denominator of disease-specific mortality?","choices":["Patients with the disease","Deaths from all causes","Patients who received treatment","The whole population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it is the number of people per 10,000 or 100,000 population dying of the disease, unlike case fatality, which uses patients. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is susceptibility bias?","choices":["Patients drop out or change groups","The assembled groups differ in ways that affect outcome","One group's outcomes are more likely to be detected","Results apply only to a narrow population"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: for example the groups may be at different points in the disease course, have other concurrent disease, or have had prior treatment. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"For which kind of disease is the case-fatality ratio most useful?","choices":["Chronic disease with survival of decades","Any disease that is very common","Acute disease where death follows quickly","Disease that never causes death"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it suits acute, short-term disease where death occurs shortly after diagnosis, so the deaths can be counted without long follow-up. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which is a source of selection bias in a cohort?","choices":["Convenience or volunteer samples","Blinded outcome assessment","Strict criteria for the outcome","Random assignment of patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with declines from among those selected and dropouts. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When is an inception cohort assembled?","choices":["After treatment has been completed","When the disease first becomes apparent","At the time of the outcome event","Once symptoms have peaked in severity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the aim is to assemble it as early as possible and as uniformly as possible. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Who takes part in a risk study?","choices":["People already sick","People in remission","People who have died","Healthy people"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the question is who goes on to develop the disease, and the outcome of interest is diagnosis. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A cancer begins in 2001. Screening finds it in 2005, but without screening it would have been diagnosed in 2009. Death occurs in 2011 either way. By how many years does survival from diagnosis appear longer with screening?","choices":["Six years","Four years","Two years","Ten years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: survival from diagnosis is 6 years with screening (2005 to 2011) against 2 without (2009 to 2011), yet death did not come any later. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the case-fatality rate?","choices":["Percent of patients with a disease who die of it","Deaths from a disease per 100,000 population","Percent of patients who improve after treatment","Percent of patients whose disease is undetectable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and because its denominator is patients with the disease it measures the severity of the disease itself. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do near-identical five-year survival rates fail to show?","choices":["When the deaths occur","How many patients were studied","Which patients had the disease","Whether the disease exists"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: conditions such as a dissecting aneurysm, lung cancer and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis can share one five-year rate while their survival curves differ greatly. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is a use of Cox proportional hazards regression?","choices":["Studying an exposure's effect after adjusting for confounders","Drawing a curve from the exact time of each death","Calculating the person-years contributed by each patient","Finding the time at which half the group has died"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it resembles Kaplan-Meier but can adjust for confounders and take account of several predictors at once. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is true of a risk factor's effect on prognosis?","choices":["It always worsens prognosis","It always improves prognosis","It need not worsen prognosis","It sets the case-fatality rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: risk factors do not necessarily represent a poorer prognosis, so being at risk of a disease and faring badly with it are separate questions. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the outcome of interest in a risk study?","choices":["Death or disability","Diagnosis of the disease","Response to treatment","Recurrence after remission"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the healthy participants are followed to see who is diagnosed, which is why rates are low and diagnosis is rare. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Of 250 patients diagnosed with a cancer, 150 are alive five years later and none were lost to follow-up. What is the five-year survival rate?","choices":["40 percent","25 percent","15 percent","60 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 150 divided by 250 is 0.60, and with no one lost to follow-up every patient is accounted for. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why can five-year survival rise after screening without anyone living longer?","choices":["Screening cures the disease in every patient","Screening lowers the number of people tested","Screening shortens the period of follow-up","Diagnosis moves earlier while death does not"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: patients live longer only with a diagnosis, so the clock starts sooner while death occurs at a similar point. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which poor prognostic factor after myocardial infarction is not a risk factor for developing coronary disease?","choices":["Physical inactivity","Increased cholesterol on the lipid panel","Ventricular arrhythmia","Hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because it arises after the infarction and predicts a worse outcome, but it is not something that leads a healthy person into coronary disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the natural history of a disease?","choices":["What occurs without intervention","What occurs once treatment begins","The pattern of spread through a community","The history the patient reports"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it serves as the untreated benchmark against which new treatments are compared. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which pair of endpoints can be used to describe prognosis?","choices":["Screening or diagnosis","Incidence or prevalence","Sensitivity or specificity","Cure or death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and these sit alongside the rates used to describe outcomes, such as five-year survival and remission. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which is a way to minimize measurement bias?","choices":["Matching the groups on age","Blinding those who assess the outcome","Restricting the sample to one clinic","Stratifying the analysis by sex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, together with strict criteria for whether the outcome occurred and equal effort to discover outcomes in all groups. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What kind of factors do prognosis studies look for?","choices":["Factors that cause the disease to begin","Factors that raise the chance of diagnosis","Factors that protect or promote progression","Factors that change a test's sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and these can differ from the factors that raise the chance of getting the disease in the first place. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does adjustment for important prognostic factors address in a prognosis study?","choices":["Whether the outcome was measured blindly","Whether patients were followed long enough","Whether other factors explain the outcome","Whether the entry criteria were objective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: groups can differ in prognosis because of some other factor rather than the one under study, and adjustment accounts for that. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which method of controlling selection bias also handles factors nobody measured?","choices":["Matching","Restriction","Randomization","Stratification"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is the only method of dealing with differences between groups whose benefits are not limited to the factors selected for consideration. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What do prognostic factors identify?","choices":["Patients with the same disease but different prognoses","Healthy people who will develop the disease","Patients with different diseases and equal prognoses","Tests that confirm a diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is what makes them useful: they separate patients who share a diagnosis into groups that fare differently. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
